-- title:
--   §2–§3.1, pp. 3–9 — stopped paths, d∞, nonanticipative functionals, ℂ_l^{0,0}, 𝔹, horizontal and vertical derivatives, ℂ_b^{1,2}; the continuous semimartingale with [X] = ∫A
-- statement:
--   This file sets up the path space and the regularity classes of Cont and Fournié's functional Itô calculus, and the probabilistic setting in which the functional Itô formula is stated.
--
--   **Paths.** Time is $[0,\infty)$ and a horizon $T$ is fixed. An $\mathbb R^d$-valued path is a map $x:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb R^d$; a matrix path is a map $v:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb R^{d\times d}$. For a time $t$:
--
--   1. the *stopped path* $x_t(u)=x(\min(u,t))$ is the restriction of $x$ to $[0,t]$ extended flat after $t$; it is also the horizontal extension $x_{t,h}$ of (4) for every $h\ge 0$;
--   2. the *vertical perturbation* $x_t^e$ ($e\in\mathbb R^d$) of (5) equals $x$ on $[0,t)$ and $x(t)+e$ from $t$ on;
--   3. the path $x_{t-}$ equals $x$ on $[0,t)$ and the left limit $x(t-)$ from $t$ on (it is $x_0$ when $t=0$);
--   4. a pair $(x,v)$ belongs to $D([0,t],\mathbb R^d)\times\mathcal S_t$ when both paths are cadlag on $[0,t]$ (right-continuous at every $u<t$, with a left limit at every $0<u\le t$) and $v(u)$ is a positive semidefinite matrix for every $u\le t$.
--
--   **The metric (9).** For pairs $(x,v)$ at time $t$ and $(x',v')$ at time $t'$,
--   $$d_\infty\big((t,x,v),(t',x',v')\big)=|t-t'|+\sup_{u\le T}\big|(x_t(u),v_t(u))-(x'_{t'}(u),v'_{t'}(u))\big|,$$
--   where the norm of a pair is the largest absolute value of its entries. "$d_\infty<\eta$" is encoded through an explicit bound on the supremum.
--
--   **Functionals.** A functional is a family $F=(F_t)$ assigning a value $F_t(x,v)$ to every path pair. It is *nonanticipative* when $F_t(x,v)=F_t(x_t,v_t)$, and *canonically measurable* (Definition 2.1) when each $F_t$, $t<T$, is measurable on $D([0,t],\mathbb R^d)\times\mathcal S_t$ for the $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal B_t$ generated by the evaluations $x(u),v(u)$, $u\le t$. Condition (10) is $F_t(x,v)=F_t(x,v_{t-})$ on $D([0,t],\mathbb R^d)\times\mathcal S_t$.
--
--   **Regularity classes** (all for $t<T$ and admissible path pairs):
--
--   1. continuity at fixed times (Definition 2.2, (11)): $F_t$ is continuous for $d_\infty$ at fixed $t$;
--   2. left-continuity, the class $\mathbb C^{0,0}_l([0,T))$ (Definition 2.4, (13)): for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $\eta>0$ with $|F_t(x,v)-F_{t-h}(x',v')|<\varepsilon$ whenever $0\le h\le t$ and $d_\infty((t,x,v),(t-h,x',v'))<\eta$;
--   3. boundedness preservation, the class $\mathbb B([0,T))$ (Definition 2.5, (14)): for every compact $K\subset\mathbb R^d$, $R>0$ and $t_0<T$ there is $C>0$ with $|F_t(x,v)|<C$ for $t\le t_0$, $x$ with values in $K$ on $[0,t]$ and $\sup_{s\le t}|v(s)|<R$.
--
--   **Derivatives.** $\mathcal DF$ is the horizontal derivative (Definition 3.1, (17)) when, for every $t<T$ and admissible $(x,v)$,
--   $$\mathcal D_tF(x,v)=\lim_{h\to0+}\frac{F_{t+h}(x_{t,h},v_{t,h})-F_t(x_t,v_t)}{h}.$$
--   $\nabla_xF$ is the vertical derivative (Definition 3.2, (19)) when $e\mapsto F_t(x_t^e,v_t)$ is differentiable at $0$ with partial derivatives $\partial_iF_t(x,v)$. $F\in\mathbb C^{1,2}_b([0,T))$ (Definition 3.6) with derivatives $(\mathcal DF,\nabla_xF,\nabla_x^2F)$ when $F$ is a nonanticipative, canonically measurable, left-continuous functional; $\mathcal DF$ is its horizontal derivative and is continuous at fixed times; $\nabla_xF$ is its vertical derivative and $\nabla_x^2F$ is the vertical derivative of $\nabla_xF$ (componentwise), both in $\mathbb C^{0,0}_l$; and $\mathcal DF,\nabla_xF,\nabla_x^2F\in\mathbb B$.
--
--   **The probabilistic setting** (§2, p. 3). The filtered probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal F,(\mathcal F_t),\mathbb P)$ satisfies the usual hypotheses (complete, all null sets in $\mathcal F_0$, right-continuous filtration). $X=X(0)+V+M$ is a continuous semimartingale in $\mathbb R^d$: $V$ is continuous, adapted, of bounded variation on every $[0,s]$ with $V(0)=0$; $M$ is continuous, adapted, with local-martingale components and $M(0)=0$; $X(0)$ is $\mathcal F_0$-measurable. $A$ is an adapted cadlag process with values in the positive semidefinite $d\times d$ matrices, integrable along paths on bounded intervals, and
--   $$[X^i,X^j](t)=\int_0^tA_{ij}(s)\,ds\qquad(3).$$
--
--   These are the objects of the functional Itô formula (Theorem 4.1) and of its lemmas.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Functionals are defined on all paths, and every condition quantifies only over path pairs that are cadlag on the relevant $[0,t]$ with positive semidefinite $v$, as in the paper. The derivatives are witnesses tied to $F$ by `HasDerivWithinAt` / `HasFDerivAt`, hence unique. The usual hypotheses use Mathlib's `Filtration.IsRightContinuous`. The quadratic covariation is the published `EthierKurtz.HasCrossVariation` (dyadic sums of products of increments converge in probability). Integrability of $A$ along paths is implied by the cadlag property and is stated explicitly. Definition 2.1's $\mathcal B_t$-measurability is part of $\mathbb C^{1,2}_b$: without it Theorem 2.7 fails (see the mission notes).
-- source:
--   Cont and Fournié, Functional Itô calculus and stochastic integral representation of martingales, arXiv:1002.2446v5, pp. 3–9, §2–§3.1, Definitions 2.1–2.5, 3.1, 3.2, 3.6, displays (3)–(5), (9)–(11), (13), (14), (17), (19)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_auto_M05_f3f59_EthierKurtz_HasCrossVariation

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter
open scoped NNReal ENNReal Topology BigOperators

namespace FunctionalIto.Formula

/-! ### Paths (§2.1, pp. 3–4) -/

/-- An `ℝ^d`-valued path on the time axis `ℝ≥0`. -/
abbrev Path (d : ℕ) := ℝ≥0 → (Fin d → ℝ)

/-- A `d × d`-matrix-valued path on the time axis `ℝ≥0`. -/
abbrev MPath (d : ℕ) := ℝ≥0 → Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℝ

/-- The path `x` stopped at `t`: `x_t` extended flat after `t`, i.e. the horizontal
extension `x_{t,h}` of (4) for every `h ≥ 0`. -/
noncomputable def stop {E : Type*} (t : ℝ≥0) (x : ℝ≥0 → E) : ℝ≥0 → E := fun u => x (min u t)

/-- The vertical perturbation `x_t^e` of (5): equal to `x` on `[0,t)`, equal to `x(t) + e`
at `t` (and, being a stopped path, after `t`). -/
noncomputable def bump {d : ℕ} (t : ℝ≥0) (x : Path d) (e : Fin d → ℝ) : Path d :=
  fun u => if u < t then x u else x t + e

/-- The path `x_{t−}` of p. 5: equal to `x` on `[0,t)` and to the left limit `x(t−)` at `t`
(and, being a stopped path, after `t`). At `t = 0` it is the stopped path `stop 0 x`. -/
noncomputable def leftStop {E : Type*} [TopologicalSpace E] (t : ℝ≥0) (x : ℝ≥0 → E) : ℝ≥0 → E :=
  fun u => if u < t then x u else Function.leftLim x t

/-- `x` is cadlag on `[0,t]`: right-continuous at every `u < t` and with a left limit at
every `0 < u ≤ t`. This only depends on `x` restricted to `[0,t]`. -/
def CadlagOn {E : Type*} [TopologicalSpace E] (t : ℝ≥0) (x : ℝ≥0 → E) : Prop :=
  (∀ u < t, ContinuousWithinAt x (Set.Ici u) u) ∧
  (∀ u ≤ t, 0 < u → ∃ l, Tendsto x (𝓝[<] u) (𝓝 l))

/-- `x` is cadlag on all of `ℝ≥0`. -/
def Cadlag {E : Type*} [TopologicalSpace E] (x : ℝ≥0 → E) : Prop :=
  (∀ u, ContinuousWithinAt x (Set.Ici u) u) ∧
  (∀ u, 0 < u → ∃ l, Tendsto x (𝓝[<] u) (𝓝 l))

/-- `(x, v) ∈ D([0,t], ℝ^d) × 𝒮_t`, where `𝒮_t = D([0,t], S_d^+)`: both paths are cadlag on
`[0,t]` and `v` takes positive semidefinite values on `[0,t]`. -/
def Admissible {d : ℕ} (t : ℝ≥0) (x : Path d) (v : MPath d) : Prop :=
  CadlagOn t x ∧ CadlagOn t v ∧ ∀ u ≤ t, (v u).PosSemidef

/-- `d_∞((t,x,v),(t',x',v')) < η` for the metric (9), with horizon `T`:
`|t − t'| + sup_{u ≤ T} |ω_{t,T−t}(u) − ω'_{t',T−t'}(u)| < η`, where `ω = (x, v)` and the
norm of a pair (vector, matrix) is the largest absolute value of its entries. The supremum
is encoded by an explicit bound `c`, so an unbounded family never satisfies it. -/
def DistLt {d : ℕ} (T t : ℝ≥0) (x : Path d) (v : MPath d) (t' : ℝ≥0) (x' : Path d)
    (v' : MPath d) (η : ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ c : ℝ, 0 ≤ c ∧ |(t : ℝ) - (t' : ℝ)| + c < η ∧ ∀ u ≤ T,
    (∀ i, |stop t x u i - stop t' x' u i| ≤ c) ∧
    (∀ i j, |stop t v u i j - stop t' v' u i j| ≤ c)

/-! ### Nonanticipative functionals (§2.2–§2.3, pp. 4–6) -/

/-- A family `F = (F_t)` of functionals of a path pair `(x, v)`, with values in `E`. -/
abbrev Functional (d : ℕ) (E : Type*) := ℝ≥0 → Path d → MPath d → E

/-- Nonanticipativity: `F_t(x, v)` only depends on `x` and `v` restricted to `[0,t]`. -/
def IsNonanticipative {d : ℕ} {E : Type*} (F : Functional d E) : Prop :=
  ∀ t x v, F t x v = F t (stop t x) (stop t v)

/-- The canonical σ-algebra `ℬ_t` on `D([0,t], ℝ^d) × D([0,t], S_d^+)`: generated by the
evaluations `(x, v) ↦ x(u)` and `(x, v) ↦ v(u)` for `u ≤ t`. -/
@[instance_reducible]
def canonicalSigma (d : ℕ) (t : ℝ≥0) :
    MeasurableSpace {p : Path d × MPath d // Admissible t p.1 p.2} :=
  ⨆ (u : ℝ≥0) (_ : u ≤ t),
    (MeasurableSpace.comap (fun p : {p : Path d × MPath d // Admissible t p.1 p.2} => p.1.1 u)
        inferInstance ⊔
      ⨆ (i : Fin d) (j : Fin d),
        MeasurableSpace.comap
          (fun p : {p : Path d × MPath d // Admissible t p.1 p.2} => p.1.2 u i j) inferInstance)

/-- Definition 2.1: each `F_t` (`t < T`) is `ℬ_t`-measurable on `D([0,t], ℝ^d) × 𝒮_t`. -/
def IsCanonicallyMeasurable {d : ℕ} (T : ℝ≥0) (F : Functional d ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ t < T, Measurable[canonicalSigma d t]
    (fun p : {p : Path d × MPath d // Admissible t p.1 p.2} => F t p.1.1 p.1.2)

/-- (10): predictable dependence on the second argument,
`F_t(x_t, v_t) = F_t(x_t, v_{t−})` for `(x, v) ∈ D([0,t], ℝ^d) × 𝒮_t`. -/
def PredictableInV {d : ℕ} (T : ℝ≥0) (F : Functional d ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ t < T, ∀ x v, Admissible t x v → F t x v = F t x (leftStop t v)

/-- Definition 2.2, (11): continuity at fixed times for `d_∞`. -/
def ContinuousAtFixedTimes {d : ℕ} (T : ℝ≥0) (G : Functional d ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ t < T, ∀ ε > (0 : ℝ), ∀ x v, Admissible t x v → ∃ η > (0 : ℝ), ∀ x' v',
    Admissible t x' v' → DistLt T t x v t x' v' η → |G t x v - G t x' v'| < ε

/-- Definition 2.4, (13): left-continuity; the class `ℂ_l^{0,0}([0,T))`.
(The case `h = 0` of (13) is continuity of `F_t` in the sup norm.) -/
def LeftContinuous {d : ℕ} (T : ℝ≥0) (G : Functional d ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ t < T, ∀ ε > (0 : ℝ), ∀ x v, Admissible t x v → ∃ η > (0 : ℝ), ∀ h ≤ t, ∀ x' v',
    Admissible (t - h) x' v' → DistLt T t x v (t - h) x' v' η →
      |G t x v - G (t - h) x' v'| < ε

/-- Definition 2.5, (14): boundedness preservation; the class `𝔹([0,T))`. -/
def BoundednessPreserving {d : ℕ} (T : ℝ≥0) (G : Functional d ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ K : Set (Fin d → ℝ), IsCompact K → ∀ R > (0 : ℝ), ∀ t₀ < T, ∃ C > (0 : ℝ),
    ∀ t ≤ t₀, ∀ x v, Admissible t x v → (∀ u ≤ t, x u ∈ K) →
      (∃ c < R, ∀ s ≤ t, ∀ i j, |v s i j| ≤ c) → |G t x v| < C

/-! ### Horizontal and vertical derivatives (§3.1, pp. 7–9) -/

/-- Definition 3.1, (17): `DF` is the horizontal derivative of `F` on `[0,T)`:
`𝒟_tF(x,v) = lim_{h→0+} (F_{t+h}(x_{t,h}, v_{t,h}) − F_t(x_t, v_t)) / h`. -/
def IsHorizDeriv {d : ℕ} (T : ℝ≥0) (F DF : Functional d ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ t < T, ∀ x v, Admissible t x v →
    HasDerivWithinAt (fun h : ℝ => F (t + h.toNNReal) (stop t x) (stop t v)) (DF t x v)
      (Set.Ici 0) 0

/-- Definition 3.2, (19): `G` is the vertical derivative (gradient) of `F` on `[0,T)`:
`e ↦ F_t(x_t^e, v_t)` is (Fréchet) differentiable at `0` and its partial derivatives are
`G_t(x, v)_i`. -/
def IsVertGrad {d : ℕ} (T : ℝ≥0) (F : Functional d ℝ) (G : Functional d (Fin d → ℝ)) :
    Prop :=
  ∀ t < T, ∀ x v, Admissible t x v → ∃ L : (Fin d → ℝ) →L[ℝ] ℝ,
    HasFDerivAt (fun e : Fin d → ℝ => F t (bump t x e) v) L 0 ∧
      ∀ i, L (Pi.single i 1) = G t x v i

/-- Definition 3.6: `F ∈ ℂ_b^{1,2}([0,T))` with horizontal derivative `DF`, vertical
derivative `DxF` and second vertical derivative `DxxF` (the vertical derivative of `DxF`,
componentwise: `DxxF_{ij} = ∂_j (DxF)_i`). `F` is a nonanticipative functional in the sense
of Definition 2.1 (nonanticipative and `ℬ_t`-measurable), left-continuous, horizontally
differentiable with `DF` continuous at fixed times, twice vertically differentiable with
`DxF, DxxF ∈ ℂ_l^{0,0}`, and `DF, DxF, DxxF ∈ 𝔹`. -/
def IsC12b {d : ℕ} (T : ℝ≥0) (F DF : Functional d ℝ) (DxF : Functional d (Fin d → ℝ))
    (DxxF : Functional d (Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℝ)) : Prop :=
  IsNonanticipative F ∧ IsCanonicallyMeasurable T F ∧ LeftContinuous T F ∧
  IsHorizDeriv T F DF ∧ ContinuousAtFixedTimes T DF ∧
  IsVertGrad T F DxF ∧ (∀ i, LeftContinuous T (fun t x v => DxF t x v i)) ∧
  (∀ i, IsVertGrad T (fun t x v => DxF t x v i) (fun t x v j => DxxF t x v i j)) ∧
  (∀ i j, LeftContinuous T (fun t x v => DxxF t x v i j)) ∧
  BoundednessPreserving T DF ∧
  (∀ i, BoundednessPreserving T (fun t x v => DxF t x v i)) ∧
  (∀ i j, BoundednessPreserving T (fun t x v => DxxF t x v i j))

/-! ### The probabilistic setting (§2, p. 3) -/

/-- The usual hypotheses: `P` is complete, every `P`-null set is in `ℱ 0`, and `ℱ` is
right-continuous. -/
def UsualHypotheses {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω)
    (ℱ : Filtration ℝ≥0 ‹MeasurableSpace Ω›) : Prop :=
  P.IsComplete ∧ (∀ N : Set Ω, MeasurableSet N → P N = 0 → MeasurableSet[ℱ 0] N) ∧
    ℱ.IsRightContinuous

/-- The standing setting of §2 (p. 3): `X = X(0) + V + M` is a continuous semimartingale
(`V` continuous, adapted, of bounded variation on compacts, `V(0) = 0`; `M` continuous,
adapted, a local martingale componentwise, `M(0) = 0`; `X(0)` is `ℱ_0`-measurable), and
`A` is an adapted cadlag process with values in the positive semidefinite matrices,
locally integrable along paths, with `[X^i, X^j](t) = ∫_0^t A_{ij}(s) ds` (3). -/
def IsContSemimartingale {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {d : ℕ} (P : Measure Ω)
    (ℱ : Filtration ℝ≥0 ‹MeasurableSpace Ω›) (X V M : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Fin d → ℝ)
    (A : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℝ) : Prop :=
  Measurable[ℱ 0] (X 0) ∧
  (∀ t ω i, X t ω i = X 0 ω i + V t ω i + M t ω i) ∧
  (∀ ω i, Continuous (fun t => V t ω i)) ∧
  (∀ i, Adapted ℱ (fun t ω => V t ω i)) ∧
  (∀ ω i (s : ℝ≥0), BoundedVariationOn (fun t => V t ω i) (Set.Icc 0 s)) ∧
  (∀ ω i, V 0 ω i = 0) ∧
  (∀ ω i, Continuous (fun t => M t ω i)) ∧
  (∀ i, Adapted ℱ (fun t ω => M t ω i)) ∧
  (∀ i, EthierKurtz.IsSourceLocalMartingale P ℱ (fun t ω => M t ω i)) ∧
  (∀ ω i, M 0 ω i = 0) ∧
  (∀ i j, Adapted ℱ (fun t ω => A t ω i j)) ∧
  (∀ ω, Cadlag (fun t => A t ω)) ∧
  (∀ t ω, (A t ω).PosSemidef) ∧
  (∀ ω i j (t : ℝ), IntervalIntegrable (fun s : ℝ => A s.toNNReal ω i j) volume 0 t) ∧
  (∀ i j, EthierKurtz.HasCrossVariation P (fun t ω => X t ω i) (fun t ω => X t ω j)
    (fun t ω => ∫ s in (0 : ℝ)..(t : ℝ), A s.toNNReal ω i j))

end FunctionalIto.Formula


